  "preview": "Maya started a dog-walking business. A startup cost, a per-walk fee, and a savings goal. How many walks until she's ahead?",
  "body": {
    "goal": "Write and solve a two-step inequality from a real-world context, then interpret the boundary value and the inequality direction in the story.",
    "studentPrompt": "Maya is starting a neighborhood dog-walking business this summer. To get going, she spends $45 on a sturdy leash kit, treats, and waste bags. She charges $8 per walk, but she also tips her younger brother $1.50 each walk for helping her hand out flyers and answer the doorbell.\n\nMaya wants to save at least $120 by the end of August to buy a new bike.\n\n1. Let w stand for the number of walks. Write an inequality that represents Maya saving at least $120 after paying her startup cost and her brother.\n2. Solve the inequality. What is the fewest number of walks Maya needs?\n3. If Maya walks dogs 4 days a week for 10 weeks, will she meet her goal? By how much will she exceed it (or miss it)?\n4. Maya is thinking about raising her price to $10 per walk. How many walks would she need now? Is it a 'small' or 'big' difference?\n5. Her brother asks for a raise to $2 per walk. Keeping the original $8 price, how does that change the answer to question 2?",
    "image": {
      "alt": "Cartoon scene showing a dog on a leash with a coin jar labeled with a goal of $120 and a small bike silhouette in the corner.",
      "svg": "<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 480 220\" font-family=\"system-ui, sans-serif\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"480\" height=\"220\" fill=\"#f6f3ea\"/><rect x=\"0\" y=\"170\" width=\"480\" height=\"50\" fill=\"#cfe1b9\"/><g transform=\"translate(80,120)\"><ellipse cx=\"30\" cy=\"45\" rx=\"35\" ry=\"10\" fill=\"#0000\" opacity=\"0.1\"/><rect x=\"5\" y=\"15\" width=\"55\" height=\"25\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#c98b6b\"/><circle cx=\"15\" cy=\"42\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#7a4a36\"/><circle cx=\"50\" cy=\"42\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#7a4a36\"/><rect x=\"50\" y=\"5\" width=\"18\" height=\"15\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#c98b6b\"/><circle cx=\"61\" cy=\"11\" r=\"1.5\" fill=\"#222\"/><polygon points=\"50,5 55,-3 58,5\" fill=\"#a06b4d\"/><polygon points=\"63,5 67,-1 68,5\" fill=\"#a06b4d\"/><path d=\"M5,22 q-8,2 -10,8\" stroke=\"#a06b4d\" stroke-width=\"3\" fill=\"none\"/></g><path d=\"M155,140 q15,-20 30,0\" stroke=\"#6b5b3a\" stroke-width=\"2\" fill=\"none\"/><g transform=\"translate(230,80)\"><path d=\"M0,30 L0,90 Q0,100 10,100 L70,100 Q80,100 80,90 L80,30 Z\" fill=\"#fff3b0\" stroke=\"#b08a2e\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><rect x=\"-5\" y=\"22\" width=\"90\" height=\"12\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#b08a2e\"/><circle cx=\"25\" cy=\"60\" r=\"7\" fill=\"#e2c044\" stroke=\"#8a6a1e\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"25\" y=\"64\" font-size=\"8\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5a4612\">$</text><circle cx=\"48\" cy=\"72\" r=\"7\" fill=\"#e2c044\" stroke=\"#8a6a1e\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"48\" y=\"76\" font-size=\"8\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5a4612\">$</text><circle cx=\"58\" cy=\"55\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#e2c044\" stroke=\"#8a6a1e\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"40\" y=\"18\" font-size=\"12\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"800\" fill=\"#1f2a44\">Goal: $120</text></g><g transform=\"translate(370,130)\"><circle cx=\"15\" cy=\"30\" r=\"12\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#3a4a6b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"55\" cy=\"30\" r=\"12\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#3a4a6b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M15,30 L35,10 L55,30\" stroke=\"#3a4a6b\" stroke-width=\"2\" fill=\"none\"/><line x1=\"35\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"40\" y2=\"5\" stroke=\"#3a4a6b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><line x1=\"15\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"30\" y2=\"30\" stroke=\"#3a4a6b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/></g><text x=\"240\" y=\"30\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#1f2a44\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Maya's Dog Walking</text></svg>"
    },
    "launch": "Tell the story conversationally — 'Imagine you're starting a small business this summer.' Don't write the inequality on the board. Send groups to whiteboards to figure out what to write and why. Resist hinting at the structure.",
    "materials": [
      "Vertical whiteboards",
      "Markers",
      "Calculator allowed"
    ],
    "concepts": [
      "two-step-inequalities",
      "writing-inequalities",
      "solving-inequalities",
      "real-world-modeling",
      "interpreting-solutions"
    ],
    "taskType": "investigation",
    "btcFit": "Strong BTC fit. Q1 is the diamond — groups will argue about whether to use >, <, >=, or whether to subtract the brother's pay from the per-walk earnings. Q3 makes the abstract answer concrete. Q4-Q5 add 'what if' pressure that exposes how the structure of the inequality responds to changes.",
    "anticipatedApproaches": "- Q1: Earnings per walk net of brother's tip: 8 - 1.50 = 6.50. So 6.50w - 45 >= 120. Some groups will write 8w - 1.50w - 45 >= 120 and that's equivalent.\n- Q2: 6.50w >= 165, so w >= 25.38... Maya needs at least 26 walks (integer ceiling).\n- Q3: 4 walks/week x 10 weeks = 40 walks. 40 x 6.50 - 45 = 260 - 45 = $215 saved. Exceeds goal by $95.\n- Q4: New net = 10 - 1.50 = 8.50. 8.50w - 45 >= 120, w >= 19.41, so 20 walks. Difference: 6 fewer walks. Many groups will call this 'big' because it's about 25% fewer.\n- Q5: New net = 8 - 2 = 6.00. 6w - 45 >= 120, w >= 27.5, so 28 walks. Three more walks than the original.",
    "commonMisconceptions": "- Forgetting to subtract the brother's tip from the per-walk earnings: writing 8w - 45 >= 120 (gets w >= 20.625, undercount).\n- Treating the $45 startup as added income rather than a cost: writing 6.50w + 45 >= 120 (gets w >= 11.5, way too few).\n- Stopping at w >= 25.38 without rounding up to the next whole walk. 'You can't do 0.38 of a walk.'\n- Using > instead of >= when the problem says 'at least.'",
    "discussionQuestions": "- Why did you round 25.38 UP instead of down? Where in the story is that decision happening?\n- Two groups wrote different inequalities (8w - 1.50w - 45 vs. 6.50w - 45). Are they equivalent? Prove it.\n- In Q4, the price went up only $2 but the walks dropped by 6. Why does a small price change matter so much?\n- If Maya's startup cost was $0 (she already had a leash), how would the inequality and answer change?",
    "extensions": "- Maya's neighbor will pay her a $30 bonus if she completes at least 30 walks in the summer. Write a new inequality that includes the bonus, and find the new minimum number of walks to reach $120. Does Maya 'gain anything' from chasing the bonus?\n- Graph the inequality 6.50w - 45 >= 120 on a number line. Why does only the part of the line w >= 26 make sense in the dog-walking story? Where would 26 fall on the math number line vs. the 'story' number line?\n- Maya is comparing herself to a friend who sells lemonade: profit = 0.75c - 18, where c is cups. If both want to save $120, who has to do more 'units'? Whose business scales better — and why?",
    "sampleSolutions": "1. Net earnings per walk = 8 - 1.50 = $6.50. Inequality: 6.50w - 45 >= 120.\n2. 6.50w >= 165, so w >= 25.385. Round up: Maya needs at least 26 walks.\n3. 40 walks x 6.50 = $260. Minus $45 startup = $215. She exceeds the $120 goal by $95.\n4. New inequality: 8.50w - 45 >= 120, so w >= 19.41. Round up to 20 walks. 6 fewer walks than before — a meaningful drop.\n5. New inequality: 6w - 45 >= 120, so w >= 27.5. Round up to 28 walks. 2 more walks than original.",
